{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reclassifies small-scale possession as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth will face the option of entering a health-focused intervention or risking up to six months in jail. The law also tightens rules around drug sales near areas like playgrounds and schools, signaling a shift toward stricter enforcement.\n\n***\n\nOfficers now have a narrow pathway to redirect people away from arrest: if the person has no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and is medically stable, police can contact a local behavioral health dispatch line. A mobile team must arrive within half an hour to assess and offer support. If they don’t show up in time, arrest becomes the default action—no delays allowed.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue the law grants local authorities flexibility to tailor responses based on available resources. Critics warn this could lead to inconsistent outcomes, where access to help depends heavily on geography and staffing. Police chiefs, like Portland’s Bob Day, stress that the window for intervention is tight, and officers can’t afford to wait indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects a broader national reckoning with addiction and public safety. While some in the country are experimenting with harm reduction, others are returning to punitive models. It’s a reminder that policies evolve—not always in harmony with the needs of those affected. Imagine someone trying to eat a meal while worrying about being stopped for possession, or shopping for groceries with the fear of a random check. The tongue of the law may speak in rules, but the heart of the matter is human dignity.\n\nP.P.S This policy affects people of eve